Below is the detailed summary of what we actually established, what changed, what was tested, what failed, and what remains open. I am separating **series-level decisions**, **Track 1-specific decisions**, **later fractal/self-similarity work**, **Suno workflow discoveries**, **the latest generation result**, and **things that must not be accidentally promoted into the blueprint**.

# SUNO MUSIC PROJECT — DETAILED WORKING SUMMARY

## 1. The original objective

This was never meant to be a one-off Suno song.

The project began as an attempt to create a **recognisable series/catalogue identity** with its own musical language, audience positioning, production philosophy and internal logic.

The original brief already contained several different categories of information:

- a three-note compositional seed;
- recursive/fractal development;
- 1970s Anatolian psychedelic ancestry;
- acoustic/analogue production;
- 9/8 Aksak rhythm;
- selected electronic genres as a wider catalogue territory;
- reference channels for positioning and audience strategy;
- late-night/home/car/work listening use cases;
- alternative/underground/sophisticated audience positioning;
- a desire to build a genuinely new sound rather than imitate trends.

The important correction from the early discussion was that the list of Deep House, Vocal House, Nu Disco, Chillout, Dub Techno, Downtempo, etc. was **not a request to combine every genre into every song**. It represented a **creative territory for the series**. fileciteturn9file3

---

# 2. The first major mistake that had to be corrected

My first interpretation collapsed the entire genre list into one supposed hybrid and proposed a genre solution such as “Anatolian Aksak Deep Psych.”

That was wrong because the user had already said:

- this is a **series**;
- this is about **creating our own unique sound**;
- the reference channels were there to be **analysed**, not copied;
- different genre influences could serve different songs and different catalogue functions.

The correct understanding became:

> **one sonic universe / catalogue identity, with different genre centres from track to track.**

The later analysis also established that the reference channels were not merely sonic references. They were relevant to:

- branding;
- catalogue architecture;
- audience targeting;
- listening context;
- channel segmentation;
- distribution strategy;
- creative philosophy;
- consistency versus variation. fileciteturn9file0

A useful distinction emerged:

**Gentleman**  
= broader curator/tastemaker/experimental umbrella.

**Deep & Elegant**  
= more focused listening proposition and sonic destination.

The project therefore needed to learn from the **functional strengths** of those channels without copying their sound.

---

# 3. The wider series strategy

The catalogue should not be a single fixed hybrid genre.

Instead:

- one song may be Vocal House-centred;
- another may be Deep House-centred;
- another may lean Chill House;
- another Downtempo;
- another could use Dub Techno spatial behaviour;
- another could use Nu Disco movement.

What carries across the catalogue is the **higher-order identity**, not a compulsory genre stack.

This distinction is critical:

> **Genre centre = song-specific.  
> Sonic philosophy = series-level.**

The project is therefore not:

> Anatolian rock + deep house + vocal house + nu disco + chillout + dub techno + downtempo in every song.

It is:

> a recognisable creative world capable of travelling through those territories.

---

# 4. The strongest early series-level production principle

One of the strongest ideas developed in the earlier work was:

> **Electronic behaviour, acoustic/analogue source.**

This did not mean “retro” or “anti-electronic.”

It meant that functions normally produced electronically could be rebuilt from physical sources and analogue processing.

Examples developed in the earlier work included:

- a house-like pad from bowed or sustained saz material, harmonics, organ/harmonium, room resonance, tape and spring ambience;
- dub-like delay depth from tape or magnetic multi-head echo;
- synthetic shaker behaviour from def/riq overdubs;
- sub weight from davul resonance and/or muted bass;
- riser behaviour from feedbacking fuzzed harmonics and analogue wah/filter movement;
- pumping or breathing from performance dynamics and physical/hardware compression rather than default software sidechain presets. fileciteturn9file18

The point was not “ban technology for purity.”

The point was:

> **translate electronic musical functions into a physical, handmade sonic language.**

That could become one of the project’s strongest identifying features.

---

# 5. What “Anatolian” was supposed to mean

A major correction happened here too.

The objective is **not** to produce obvious Turkish folk fusion.

The project does **not** want a dense stack of:

- oud;
- kanun;
- darbuka;
- saz everywhere;
- obvious regional ornamentation;
- “Middle Eastern” presets;
- bazaar/hammam/world-music scenery.

The useful inheritance from 1960s/70s Anatolian psychedelia is primarily its **invention philosophy**:

- hybridisation;
- local adaptation;
- raw analogue character;
- modal/microtonal bends;
- fuzz;
- human imperfection;
- unusual rhythmic language;
- rebuilding Western/electric functions through available local instruments;
- finding a unique sonic solution rather than copying an existing genre literally.

The ideal result was described conceptually as:

> **globally contemporary on the surface, culturally rooted underneath.**

Or:

> **placeless on the surface, culturally rooted underneath.**

That is very different from making an obvious “Anatolian House” track.

---

# 6. The desired audience-facing sonic world

The intended surface experience was consistently:

- elegant;
- emotionally rich;
- warm;
- sophisticated;
- hypnotic;
- late-night;
- human;
- alternative;
- atmospheric;
- suitable for long listening;
- good for night driving;
- home;
- headphones;
- car;
- work/focus.

It should not become:

- dark peak-time club music;
- festival EDM;
- generic lounge;
- obvious world-music fusion;
- highly polished generic Suno deep house;
- aggressive psych rock.

The sound should have enough accessibility to live comfortably in playlists, while still carrying a recognisable non-default identity.

The original brief explicitly tied the project to late nights, night drives, relaxation, focus, home/car/work listening, sophistication and emotional/sonic richness. fileciteturn9file3

---

# 7. Track 1 was a specific experiment, not the entire blueprint

The first serious track experiment chose **one genre centre**:

> **underground/elegant Vocal House**

with a specific rhythmic invention:

> **120 BPM house behaviour inside true 9/8 Aksak, grouped 2+2+2+3.**

The important point was to create a house-like bodily continuity **without straightening the rhythm into 4/4**.

Track 1’s provisional system was:

- core ancestry: 1970s Anatolian psychedelic character;
- genre encounter: underground/elegant Vocal House;
- metre: 9/8 Aksak;
- grouping: 2+2+2+3;
- house-like low anchors;
- analogue/acoustic production;
- female melismatic vocal;
- one selected melisma: **AMAN**;
- fuzz saz;
- physical room and analogue spatial depth;
- minimal lyrics.

This was a **Track 1 grammar**, not a permanent rule for all future songs. fileciteturn9file12

---

# 8. The Track 1 rhythmic engine

The most distinctive rhythmic idea was:

**Davul anchors:**

1 – 3 – 5 – 7

inside:

**1-2 | 3-4 | 5-6 | 7-8-9**

This creates:

- 1→3 = two eighth notes;
- 3→5 = two;
- 5→7 = two;
- 7→next 1 = three.

So the body hears:

> **steady → steady → steady → stretch**

This became more accurately described as:

> **an asymmetrical four-anchor house illusion**

rather than genuine four-on-the-floor.

The final stretched cell is precisely what prevents the groove from becoming ordinary 4/4.

That asymmetry was considered one of the most promising inventions in the Track 1 design. fileciteturn9file12

---

# 9. Pulse 9

Another Track 1 idea was a very low tuned davul/sub-like resonance on **pulse 9**.

The purpose was to turn the stretched 7-8-9 cell into a pull toward the next bar:

> 7 — 8 — low boom → 1

Two versions were deliberately left open for testing:

**Version A**  
pulse-9 boom every bar  
= stronger identity, more radical.

**Version B**  
pulse-9 boom every second or fourth bar  
= more restrained/elegant.

This was explicitly something to test rather than settle theoretically. fileciteturn9file12

---

# 10. High-frequency rhythm

Def and riq were not supposed to function as generic “ethnic percussion.”

They were intended to provide the **high-frequency continuum** normally supplied by hats/shakers in electronic music.

The key correction was:

> **not trap hi-hats.**

Instead:

- restrained house-like subdivision;
- fine continuous nocturnal motion;
- tiny Aksak inflection in the final three-pulse cell;
- enough asymmetry to remind the body that the cycle is still 2+2+2+3.

So the groove hierarchy became:

**LOW**  
davul 1/3/5/7

**EXTRA LOW**  
pulse-9 resonance

**MID TRANSIENT**  
darbuka around 4/8

**HIGH**  
def/riq continuum with subtle 3-cell signature. fileciteturn9file12

---

# 11. The unresolved Track 1 bass question

One decision was deliberately left open.

Should Track 1 use:

### A. Davul-only low end

Advantages:

- stranger;
- more original;
- more minimal;
- more radical;
- makes the percussion itself the bass engine.

Or:

### B. Muted electric bass + davul

Advantages:

- more seductive;
- more immediately compatible with Vocal House;
- greater harmonic movement;
- stronger bridge to the reference audience.

This was never conclusively settled.

It should therefore **not be silently fixed later**. fileciteturn9file12

---

# 12. Track 1 vocal concept

Another focused decision was to avoid a huge soulful-house diva vocal.

The vocal was intended to be:

- female;
- intimate;
- raw;
- low mezzo or contralto territory;
- close-miked;
- audible breath;
- slight chest texture;
- restrained ornamentation;
- low-volume;
- nearly whispered at onset;
- opening into an actual pitched sustained tone;
- naturally imperfect;
- no autotune;
- no glossy belting.

The reason for avoiding a literal full whisper was that a true whisper loses pitch definition and would weaken the melisma.

The chosen Track 1 vocal object was:

> **AMAN**

Not AMAN + EYVAH + YAA + AHHH all together.

The choice of a single melisma was intended to make it a **recognisable sonic object**, not a random collection of Turkish interjections. fileciteturn9file12

---

# 13. The first fractal model

The earliest fractal idea was highly explicit and mechanical:

- one three-note motif;
- repetitions at multiple time scales;
- each descendant shorter;
- softer;
- brighter;
- same contour;
- vocal echo branching 1→2→4;
- rainfall as macro-arrangement metaphor.

This was a useful first experiment because it translated fractal thinking into concrete musical operations.

The original brief explicitly linked:

- three-note motif → compositional mechanism;
- shorter/softer/brighter → recursive transformation;
- echo splitting → fractal vocal processing;
- rainfall → macro-arrangement. fileciteturn9file3

At that stage, the project explored:

- larger ancestor;
- smaller descendants;
- motif in saz;
- motif in vocal;
- motif in percussion;
- motif in echo;
- same contour at different scales.

---

# 14. Rainfall was an implementation model, not the ultimate concept

Rainfall became one specific arrangement metaphor:

- First Drop;
- Isolated Rain;
- Pattern Recognition;
- Rainfall;
- Downpour;
- Dissolution;
- Second Rain;
- Last Drop.

The track would grow from isolated events into density and then dissolve.

This was a useful **Track 1-era implementation of fractal thinking**, but it was never the only possible form of self-similarity.

Later discussion explicitly broadened the idea.

Fractals/self-similarity do not have to sound like rain.

They could theoretically resemble:

- flame;
- cracks;
- leaves;
- branching;
- waves;
- wind;
- fracture patterns;
- other recursive natural or structural processes.

Therefore:

> **Rainfall is not the blueprint.**

It was one earlier compositional metaphor.

---

# 15. The fractal concept then evolved significantly

The project later recognised that the original strict model:

> 1→2→4  
> shorter→softer→brighter

was too deterministic to describe the more interesting musical behaviour we wanted.

The concept evolved into something closer to **inheritance**.

The ancestor remains recognisable, but descendants do not simply perform a fixed transformation menu.

Instead:

> **seed → environment → adapted descendant → changed environment → next adapted descendant**

This was the major conceptual advance.

---

# 16. “Reverse Déjà Vu”

A useful phrase emerged for the desired listening effect:

> **Reverse Déjà Vu**

The concept:

You recognise that something belongs to the same musical ancestry, but it is not a literal repetition.

Each new form returns:

- related;
- altered;
- context-sensitive;
- carrying the memory of previous generations;
- shaped by everything already sounding.

The listener experiences:

> **familiar without repeating.**

This became more important than mechanical recursion.

---

# 17. Current self-similarity model

The current model is:

### Identity

There is an ancestor/seed.

### Coexistence

Older generations may remain audible when new ones arrive.

### Inheritance

New forms retain recognisable ancestry.

### Environmental adaptation

A new form is influenced by the harmonic, rhythmic and textural environment created by what came before.

### Cumulative causality

Every new generation changes the environment for the next.

### No identical return

The piece remains internally related without ordinary looping.

This was sometimes described as:

> **self-similar polyphony**

and:

> **a family of related forms rather than duplicated copies.**

Important:

This compositional mechanism should **not replace the sound-design project**.

It is one powerful layer inside it.

---

# 18. The key hierarchy we later lost

The intended hierarchy should have remained:

### Level 1 — New sonic identity

The actual sound world we are trying to establish.

### Level 2 — Song-specific genre centre

Vocal House, Deep House, Chill House, etc.

### Level 3 — Psychedelic/invention DNA

Analogue behaviour, modal ambiguity, rawness, sonic mutation, physical experimentation.

### Level 4 — Compositional mechanism

Fractal/self-similar/Reverse Déjà Vu behaviour when appropriate.

### Level 5 — Lyrics and vocal identity

Human song content.

The mistake later was spending so much time on Level 4 that Levels 1–3 became underprotected.

---

# 19. The later Deep House / Chill House experiment

After the earlier Vocal House/Aksak work, a later experiment moved toward a **Deep House / Chill House-centred song**.

The long Lyrics-section concept used language such as:

- warm;
- elegant;
- relaxed;
- late-night;
- smooth;
- hypnotic;
- spacious;
- soulful restraint;
- organic analogue character;
- globally contemporary;
- difficult to place geographically;
- suited to night driving/home/headphones;
- subtle 1970s Anatolian psychedelic invention;
- modal bends;
- imperfect human phrasing;
- restrained female melisma.

Then it appended the newer self-similarity law:

- one small three-note seed;
- related generations at different scales/positions/registers/forms;
- later generations shaped by earlier generations;
- older generations remain;
- self-similar polyphony;
- familiar without repeating.

This was not supposed to replace the earlier blueprint.

It was a later **test implementation**.

---

# 20. Important Suno workflow discovery: Lyrics Assistant

A major practical discovery was that Suno’s Lyrics Assistant/Lyricist often understood the **conceptual compositional law** much better than the Create Style function.

When asked to interpret the long concept, it understood ideas such as:

- tiny three-note idea;
- everything grows from that seed;
- motif returns in changed forms;
- older forms remain present;
- new forms are affected by what already happened;
- generations overlap;
- inheritance and variation;
- each layer changes the ground for the next.

This was important evidence that Suno could understand the conceptual architecture at the **Lyrics interpretation level**.

---

# 21. Important Suno workflow discovery: Create Style

Create Style behaved differently.

It often turned the same conceptual material into:

- genre;
- BPM;
- instrumentation;
- production;
- general mood;
- arrangement vocabulary.

It repeatedly **failed to preserve the unusual compositional causality**.

It might retain:

- three-note motif;
- recursive language;
- variants;
- layered generations.

But it often lost:

- older generations influencing later ones;
- environment changing;
- causal inheritance;
- adaptation;
- “familiar without repeating.”

Instead, it tended to replace those ideas with more conventional implementation vocabulary such as:

- transposition;
- stretching;
- reversing;
- fragmentation;
- phasing;
- granular delay;
- polyrhythms;
- counterpoint;
- instrument lists.

This distinction became extremely important.

---

# 22. The emerging Suno division of labour

The practical conclusion became:

### Lyrics section

Can carry:

- unusual compositional law;
- structure;
- conceptual architecture;
- lyrical content;
- performance instructions.

### Lyricist

Useful for checking whether Suno actually understands the conceptual idea.

### Create Style

Useful for discovering how Suno wants to translate the overall material into:

- genre;
- sonic palette;
- instrumentation;
- production language;
- arrangement character.

### Audio generation

The final authority.

We should not keep theorising indefinitely.

The real test is what comes out.

---

# 23. “Listen first, act second”

A useful workflow principle emerged:

> **We do not know exactly what Suno understood until we ask it and then hear the result.**

Therefore:

1. give Suno the concept;
2. ask Lyricist what it thinks the concept means;
3. inspect Create Style;
4. generate;
5. listen;
6. identify demonstrated failures;
7. only correct those failures;
8. use successful audio as the next reference.

This is much better than endlessly rewriting prompts based on speculation.

---

# 24. Three-account testing logic

The project also used multiple Suno accounts to test behaviour before committing main-account credits.

Different accounts/modes sometimes interpreted the same conceptual material differently.

Examples observed in earlier testing included:

- one generation drifting into indie-electronic territory;
- another closer to the recursive conceptual idea;
- another drifting toward alt-R&B/Rhodes/guitar/sub-bass.

This reinforced the point that **Suno interpretation is variable** and must be tested empirically rather than treated as deterministic.

---

# 25. The lyric problem

We eventually realised that the sound experiment had become too abstract because the song lacked enough **actual lyrical identity**.

The project had spent a long time establishing sound and architecture but had not sufficiently accounted for how the actual lyrics influence Suno’s musical casting.

That became an important breakthrough.

The user’s lyrical catalogue is largely experience-based and includes:

- survival;
- toxic relationship aftermath;
- introspection;
- emotional observation;
- personal imagery;
- layered meaning;
- wordplay;
- occasional humour/double meaning.

The strategy became:

> use existing songs as a source library rather than generate generic Deep House lyrics from scratch.

Long songs can be distilled into:

- central idea;
- strongest image;
- characteristic turn of phrase;
- 2–4 line micro-lyric.

---

# 26. “Signal in the Rain” lyric mining

One existing song supplied for mining included imagery such as:

- static;
- ringing phone;
- streetlights;
- taxi windows;
- blue light;
- erased message;
- thunder;
- signal/noise;
- dancing through pain.

A three-line micro-lyric was then extracted:

> streetlights bending on the glass  
> every signal finds its way  
> i'm still dancing in the rain

This was intended to be:

- minimal;
- more uplifting;
- enough actual lyric to let Suno cast the music as a song;
- not an explicit explanation of the fractal concept.

---

# 27. The lyric-vocabulary problem

However, this later exposed another issue.

We had already said we wanted to avoid repeatedly using the same generic/AI-saturated semantic field.

The three-line lyric concentrated:

- streetlights;
- signal;
- rain.

Even though these came from the user’s own previous lyric, together they reproduced a familiar late-night electronic-song vocabulary we were trying to move away from.

So that lyric seed should **not automatically be treated as final**.

It demonstrated something useful about Suno, but it may not be the right lyric for the finished song.

---

# 28. A/B discovery: lyrics materially change Create Style

This was one of the most useful practical observations.

Before inserting the actual three-line lyric, Create Style produced something like:

- warm late-night deep groove;
- smooth hypnotic pulse;
- analogue character;
- Rhodes;
- muted electric piano;
- analogue bass;
- brushed drums;
- hand percussion;
- Anatolian psych colours;
- spring/tape ambience.

After inserting the actual three-line lyric, Create Style became much more musically committed:

- Warm late-night Deep House;
- immediate full-intensity opening;
- hypnotic four-on-the-floor;
- analogue drum machines;
- rounded Moog bass;
- Rhodes chords;
- muted electric guitar;
- modal/microtonal bends;
- saz accents;
- tape saturation;
- spring reverb;
- night-drive groove.

That was important.

Only three lyric lines materially changed Suno’s interpretation.

---

# 29. The big workflow insight from the lyrics experiment

The emerging lesson was:

> We may not need to manually author every conventional Style decision in advance.

If:

- the creative world is well defined;
- the unusual architecture is protected;
- the lyrics belong naturally to the song;

then Suno can infer a significant amount of:

- instrumentation;
- vocal treatment;
- mood;
- production;
- genre casting.

This could reduce over-engineering.

But this discovery required an important qualification:

> **Suno can automate ordinary stylistic decisions only after we protect the non-default identity.**

We should not ask it to invent the identity itself.

---

# 30. The first actual generation from the current test

The generation came out at only about **40 seconds**.

That meant there was not enough chronological space for:

- growth;
- accumulation;
- self-similar development;
- harmonic/environmental adaptation;
- any meaningful “bloom.”

So duration/structural space became a demonstrated problem.

---

# 31. The actual sonic result

The user’s listening feedback was:

- too short;
- did not have time to bloom;
- not psychedelic enough;
- leaned more toward **Nu Disco**;
- later Suno’s compressed style summary was observed as something like:

> **“funk, psychedelic, late-night groove”**

That last observation was especially important.

---

# 32. The word “groove” became a diagnostic issue

The project had used **groove** extensively as a positive word.

But Suno’s compressed interpretation appears to have promoted it toward:

> funk

That could explain part of the Nu Disco/funky drift.

The issue is not that “groove” is forbidden forever.

The issue is that in this specific context, repeated use of the word may be steering Suno toward a stylistic category we do not want to dominate.

So it became a **prompt-variable to treat cautiously**.

---

# 33. Psychedelic character was under-specified in behaviour

Another important diagnosis:

The Style output had things like:

- saz accents;
- modal bends;
- microtonal colour.

But that is **psychedelic instrumentation/colour**, not necessarily psychedelic **behaviour**.

The project’s intended psychedelia is broader:

- unstable perception of repetition;
- evolving spatial depth;
- analogue mutation;
- timbral instability;
- saturation;
- modal tension;
- fuzz behaviour;
- phase-like motion;
- sounds bleeding into one another;
- handmade experimental transformation;
- perception of familiar material becoming strange.

So:

> **psychedelic ≠ simply add saz.**

And:

> **new sound ≠ genre + unusual instrument.**

---

# 34. Style Influence

Another correction was that we were attempting to create a **new sound**, yet Style Influence had effectively been left too close to default behaviour.

The user correctly pointed out that when deliberately trying to establish a non-default identity, Style Influence should be **higher than default**.

Not necessarily maximum.

But high enough that Suno respects the deliberately designed identity rather than resolving everything into a familiar default Deep House / Nu Disco / Funky House solution.

This is an important current test variable.

---

# 35. Why the 40-second generation should not trigger a total redesign

The 40-second result was evidence of a few specific issues.

It did **not** prove that the entire existing blueprint was wrong.

Demonstrated issues were:

- insufficient duration/structural space;
- Nu Disco/funky drift;
- psychedelic behaviour too weak;
- risky “groove” wording;
- lyric vocabulary problem;
- Style Influence too low for the purpose.

Those should be corrected selectively.

The mistake made afterward was trying to reconstruct the whole project from scratch instead of treating them as **targeted generation feedback**.

---

# 36. What must NOT be accidentally promoted into the permanent blueprint

The following are **historical experiments or track-specific devices**, not universal series laws:

### Rainfall

Not mandatory.

### 1→2→4 echo splitting

Not mandatory.

### Shorter → softer → brighter

Not mandatory.

### Exact three-note motif in every song

Not yet established as mandatory series logo.

### 9/8 Aksak

Track-specific unless deliberately reused.

### Davul 1/3/5/7

Track-specific.

### Pulse-9 boom

Track-specific.

### AMAN

Track-specific.

### Fuzz saz in every song

Not mandatory.

### Vocal House

Track-specific genre centre.

### Deep House

Another possible track centre, not the catalogue definition.

### Nu Disco

Part of wider territory, but the latest drift toward it was not intentional for this test.

### Rain / storm / raindrops

Not current compositional law.

This distinction is crucial.

---

# 37. What currently looks like genuine series-level DNA

The most defensible higher-order identity developed across the work is:

### 1. A catalogue, not a one-song genre recipe

Different tracks can occupy different electronic centres.

### 2. Originality through translation and invention

Do not simply reproduce genre presets.

Rebuild musical functions through unexpected physical/analogue means.

### 3. Electronic behaviour with physical/analogue character

The music can behave like contemporary electronic music without being sonically dependent on generic digital presets.

### 4. Anatolian psychedelic inheritance as method, not scenery

Modal tension, rawness, analogue experimentation, human imperfection, hybridisation, invention.

### 5. Contemporary global surface

Not obviously geographically labelled.

### 6. Sophisticated listening context

Late-night, home, driving, headphones, focus, long listening.

### 7. Human imperfection

Avoid sterile, excessively quantised, hyper-polished generation.

### 8. Psychedelic behaviour

Not just psychedelic instrumentation.

### 9. Strong song identity

The music still needs actual lyric/vocal content capable of making Suno understand it as a song rather than a technical demonstration.

### 10. Experimental compositional language

Self-similarity, inheritance, adaptation and transformation can become part of the project’s advanced composition vocabulary—but should not overpower the sound itself.

---

# 38. The current self-similarity rule, stated cleanly

The current rule is **not**:

> repeat the same motif and make it smaller.

It is:

> Begin with a recognisable musical ancestor. Let later material inherit enough of its identity to remain related, while adapting to the harmonic, rhythmic, timbral and spatial environment created by everything already present. Earlier generations may remain audible, so every new generation changes the context for the next. The listener should recognise ancestry without hearing literal repetition.

Compactly:

> **seed → environment → adapted descendant → changed environment → next adapted descendant**

Perceptually:

> **familiar without repeating.**

That is the current form of the fractal idea.

---

# 39. The current Suno methodology

The most useful workflow established so far is:

### Step 1 — Define the song’s actual creative objective

Not the whole catalogue again.

### Step 2 — Choose one genre centre

Do not stack all permitted catalogue genres.

### Step 3 — Protect the non-default sound identity

Especially the parts Suno would otherwise resolve generically.

### Step 4 — Put unusual compositional law in Lyrics

Because the Lyrics Assistant has shown better understanding of it than Create Style.

### Step 5 — Use actual lyrics

Because actual lyric content materially affects Suno’s style synthesis.

### Step 6 — Ask Lyricist what it understands

Do not assume.

### Step 7 — Let Create Style propose implementation

Treat its output as Suno’s interpretation, not as authoritative truth.

### Step 8 — Set Style Influence appropriately

Higher than default when establishing the new identity.

### Step 9 — Generate

Do not endlessly polish the prompt before hearing the audio.

### Step 10 — Diagnose audio failures separately

For example:

- sound identity;
- genre drift;
- vocal;
- structure;
- psychedelic character;
- compositional behaviour;
- duration.

### Step 11 — Change only demonstrated failures

Do not rebuild the whole system after every imperfect generation.

### Step 12 — Use successful audio as reference

Eventually the actual successful song becomes more useful than theoretical descriptions.

---

# 40. Current known failures from the latest generation

The first current generation demonstrated:

### Duration failure

~40 seconds.

### Structural failure

No chance to bloom/develop.

### Genre drift

Too much Nu Disco/funk.

