# 21. Important Suno workflow discovery: Create Style

Create Style behaved differently.

It often turned the same conceptual material into:

- genre;
- BPM;
- instrumentation;
- production;
- general mood;
- arrangement vocabulary.

It repeatedly **failed to preserve the unusual compositional causality**.

It might retain:

- three-note motif;
- recursive language;
- variants;
- layered generations.

But it often lost:

- older generations influencing later ones;
- environment changing;
- causal inheritance;
- adaptation;
- “familiar without repeating.”

Instead, it tended to replace those ideas with more conventional implementation vocabulary such as:

- transposition;
- stretching;
- reversing;
- fragmentation;
- phasing;
- granular delay;
- polyrhythms;
- counterpoint;
- instrument lists.

This distinction became extremely important.

